\details{
What gets plotted depends on the model, because the natural quantity differs:
\itemize{
\item A \strong{GAM} is shown as the \emph{partial effect} of the smooth
-- the term's own contribution, centered to average zero. This is the
quantity this package drew before it handled anything but GAMs, and it
remains the default so existing code is unaffected.
\item \strong{Any other model}, and a GAM asked for \code{scale = "response"}, is shown as
\emph{predicted values}: the model's fitted output as \code{var} varies, with the
other predictors held at representative values.
}

The y-axis label reports which one you got. Do not compare a partial effect
against a prediction as though they were on the same footing -- one is
centered on zero and excludes the rest of the model, the other is not and
does not.

The default ribbon is a \strong{95\% pointwise interval}, matching
\code{\link[mgcv:plot.gam]{mgcv::plot.gam()}}, which draws \verb{+/- 2 SE}, and \code{\link[gratia:draw]{gratia::draw()}}, which draws
95\%. Earlier versions of this package drew \verb{+/- 1 SE} -- roughly 68\%, half
the width of both -- which a reader seeing a ribbon on a smooth would very
likely misread as 95\%. Pass \code{interval = "se"} for that narrower band, now
that asking for it is explicit.

For a GAM smooth, \code{interval = "simultaneous"} draws a band covering the
whole curve rather than each point separately. A pointwise interval covers
the true value at each x with the stated probability \emph{at that x}; across a
curve evaluated at a hundred points, the true function strays outside it far
more often than the stated rate. Any claim about the \emph{shape} of a smooth --
which is usually why one is drawn -- is a claim about the whole curve, and
the simultaneous band is the one that supports it. It is noticeably wider,
which is the point.

A \strong{Bayesian fit} (\pkg{brms}, \pkg{rstanarm}) is summarised from posterior
draws, which yield an interval but no standard error, so the ribbon is the
credible interval at \code{level} -- there is no SE ribbon to be had. Write
\code{interval = "cri"} if you would rather say so explicitly; it computes the
same thing. \pkg{brms} takes \code{re_formula} rather than \code{re.form}, and that
translation is handled for you.

A \strong{factor-smooth interaction}, \code{s(x, by = f)}, is one smooth per level of
\code{f}. Those are drawn as separate coloured curves with a legend, rather than
joined end to end into a single zigzagging line.

For a \strong{mixed model}, \code{re.form} defaults to \code{NA}, so the effect is drawn at
the population level. This matters: left to the backend's own default, the
grouping factor is held at its modal level and the plot silently shows the
effect for one arbitrary group rather than the average one. Pass
\code{re.form = NULL} to include the random effects. Note that the ribbon
reflects uncertainty in the fixed effects only -- it does not widen to
account for variation between groups.

On the response scale, \code{interval = "ci"} is built on the link scale and
back-transformed, so it stays within the range the response admits -- a
probability band will not run past 0 or 1. \code{interval = "se"} cannot use that
construction, since an asymmetric interval has no single standard error
behind it. Prefer \code{"ci"} when plotting probabilities.
